# acf-whip secret lint: one POSIX extended regex per line (grep -E). Lines starting with '#' and
# blank lines are ignored. Shared by skills/lib/bin/acfw-spec and the shipped-file checks in tests.
#
# A match is a credential LITERAL in a file that gets committed: a spec-tool declaration, an agent
# prompt, a repo-local driver, a tracked .mcp.json or .env, or a plan or task about to be sent to a
# tool. Every pattern is shape plus length, so prose that merely names a variable (GAPTRAP_API_KEY,
# a ${TOKEN} reference, "Bearer <key>") never matches and a pasted key always does.
# When one matches, acfw-spec prints the file and line (never the value) and exits 20.
#
# Scope directives. A line that is exactly `#@scope config` makes the patterns after it apply only to
# committed CONFIGURATION (declarations, agent prompts, repo drivers, tracked .mcp.json and .env);
# `#@scope all` switches back. Plan, task and workbench text is checked with the `all` patterns only.
# Why: a config-shaped pattern is a good signal in .mcp.json and noise in a spec. An API plan is full
# of JSON examples whose field names end in Key or Token ("partitionKey", "nextPageToken",
# "idempotencyKey"), and refusing those would make every block tool passable only by override. Both
# scopes redact what the engine echoes, and a reader that ignores the directive (it is a comment)
# simply applies every pattern everywhere, which only ever checks more.
#@scope all
# GapTrap MCP keys.
gtmcp_[A-Za-z0-9_-]{40,}
# OpenAI-style and most "sk-" API keys.
(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])sk-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}
# NAME=value assignments whose value is a literal (a $VAR or ${VAR} reference does not match).
(KEY|TOKEN|SECRET|PASSWORD)=["']?[^$[:space:]"'#]{16,}
# Bearer tokens written inline (for example in a tracked .mcp.json headers block).
Bearer[[:space:]]+[A-Za-z0-9._~+/-]{16,}
# Well-known provider shapes.
ghp_[A-Za-z0-9]{36}
github_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_]{50,}
AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}
xox[baprs]-[A-Za-z0-9-]{10,}
-----BEGIN [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY-----
#@scope config
# JSON key/value pairs whose key names a credential and whose value is a literal: the .mcp.json shape
# ("X-Api-Key": "...", "OPENROUTER_API_KEY": "...", "Authorization": "..."), which the NAME=value
# pattern above never sees because JSON has no '='. A value holding '$' is a ${VAR} reference
# (including "Bearer ${VAR}") and does not match. Config scope only: see the directive note above.
"[A-Za-z0-9_-]*([Kk][Ee][Yy]|[Tt][Oo][Kk][Ee][Nn]|[Ss][Ee][Cc][Rr][Ee][Tt]|[Pp][Aa][Ss][Ss][Ww][Oo][Rr][Dd]|[Aa]uthorization)"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"[^"$]{16,}"
